    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d180-a3">

                <pb facs="0341" n="269" xml:id="pg269"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 3 in No. 351.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Lowell to Mr.
                            Barrows</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1882-04-14"><hi rendition="#italic">April</hi> 14,
                        1882</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p rendition="#flush"><hi rendition="#small-caps">B. H. Barrows</hi>, Esq.,<lb/><hi
                        rendition="#italic">United States Consul, Dublin, Ireland:</hi></p>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have to acknowledge the reception of your
                    letter of the 12th, inclosing communications from William Brophy, at present
                    confined in Naas jail, with a copy of his naturalization certificate, and from
                    John L. Gannon, imprisoned in Galway.</p>
                <p>I shall give my attention to these cases. Meanwhile will you kindly inform me the
                    cases stated in the warrant for Brophy’s arrest.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>J. R. LOWELL.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
